<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n1419" root="انى" body-key="A">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَتَيْتُهُ آنِيَةً بَعْدَ آنِيَةٍ</span></b>  is a phrase mentioned by
AAF, meaning <i>I came to him time after time:</i>
in which, [says ISd,] I am of opinion that <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">آنية</span> is
of the measure <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">فَاعِلَةٌ</span> from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الإنَى</span>: but the word
commonly known is <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">آوِنَة</span> [pl. of <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَوَانٌ</span>; or <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">آيِنَة</span>,
which is syn. with <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">آوِنَة</span>: see <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَوَانٌ</span>]. (M.)

